### 8.1 What exists, and what each piece owns

| Piece | Owns |
|---|---|
| `pi/api/main.py` | public FastAPI app: `/api/status`, `/api/ready`, `/api/health` (liveness only), `/search`, `/board`, `/files`; mounts the admin and public arcade routers; bumps `session_epoch` at start |
| `pi/api/ark_health.py` | **the only place that decides readiness**: path helpers read at call time, content facts, cached loopback probe, **eight** per-section builders each with `ready`/`reason`/`action`/`content` (**six** of them blocking), measured `progress()`, measured `storage_breakdown()` + `card_facts()`, `_scan_copies()`/`copies()`/`copies_unreadable()`/`_age_hours()`/`_ago()` + `backup_section()` (the **Latest copy** row: never blocking, a `.partial` is not a copy, a copy the pages may not open is reported and never counted as "none", an unreadable age is prose and never `0`), `files_section()` counting the files a reader can **open** (`files`/`files_missing`/`uploads`, `files_missing: None` when the folder cannot be listed), `report()` |
| `pi/bin/ark-net-watch` | **the only place that decides the network after boot**: pure `decide()`, `--dry-run` observer, counters in `net-watch.state`, decisions in `net-watch.log`, acts by calling `ark-mode` |
| `pi/systemd/ark-net-watch.service` / `.timer` | the clock for the above: `oneshot` every 60 s after a 3-minute boot delay; it acts through `ark-mode`, never by itself |
| `pi/bin/ark-mode` | the one writer of `/etc/ark/mode.pinned` (`home`/`hotspot` pin, `auto` unpins, `hotspot --auto` leaves no pin) and the only thing the watchdog calls |
| `pi/api/ark_db.py` | one connection policy (`read()` / `tx()`, always closed), numbered migrations in one transaction, audit helper, install marker, `SCHEMA_VERSION` |
| `pi/api/ark_security.py` | scrypt hashing, length-only policy (12…1024, reject never truncate), opaque sessions (SHA-256 digest of a 256-bit token), CSRF, per-account + per-IP throttling |
| `pi/api/ark_admin.py` | `/admin` routes: login, forced setup, password, accounts, reset/enable/disable, audit, logout, overview, **Community** (switches + board/file moderation), **Games** (shelf, licences, suggestions, hide/show, both arcade switches), **`/admin/health`** ("What is working") |
| `pi/api/ark_storage.py` | the only write path for shared files: display name vs stored name, spool in `drop/tmp`, `os.link` finalise, size cap + free-space reserve, orphan-temp reconcile; and `present_names()` — the one reader of "what is really in the folder" (a set, or `None` when it cannot be listed), used by the listing and by `ark_health` |
| `pi/api/ark_arcade.py` / `ark_arcade_install.py` / `ark_arcade_web.py` | the game shelf: shelf rules and bounded saves, the only game download path, and the public/player pages + save API. Player names are separate tables and a separate cookie; `:8445` is the only origin that can set a save or issue that cookie. Full detail in §8.11 |
| `pi/api/ark_recover.py` | recovery logic, reached only through the root wrapper |
| `pi/bin/ark_bundle.py` | what a bundle **is**: layout, manifest format 1, path rules (`safe_rel`/`inside`), sha256, `verify_bundle()`, `copy_database()` (SQLite `backup()`, never `cp`); both tools import it so they cannot disagree |
| `pi/bin/ark-backup` | the only writer of a bundle: `--to`, `--keep 2`, `--no-files`, `--dry-run`, `--list`, `--verify`, `--quiet`; `.partial` → rename; never writes to `drop/files`; hands every published bundle (and the `.lock`) to the destination folder's owner, because the pages that report copies run as `pi` and `sudo` would otherwise leave a `root:root` copy they cannot open |
| `pi/bin/ark-restore` | the one deliberate writer of records: proves every byte first, quiesces every writer (`--no-service` for a card in a reader) and restarts what it stopped even on failure, refuses an occupied box without `--force`, never replaces a shared file, keeps the import list off an empty shelf (`.from-backup`), refuses a path that leaves the box |
| `pi/steps/95-backup.sh` | installs both tools + the sheet, creates `/srv/ark/backup` `0700`, proves `--dry-run`, prints the restore command; **no timer, on purpose** |
| `pi/bin/ark-import-books` | the book watcher: scans `drop/files` + `calibre-incoming`, imports once per sha256 into `.imported.json`, moves incoming files to `done/`, copies nothing out of the File Box |
| `pi/systemd/ark-book-import.path` / `.service` | the trigger for the above: `PathChanged` on both folders, `oneshot`, runs as `pi`, 30-minute ceiling |
| `pi/bin/ark-tls-cert` | the box's own certificate: SANs, `--show` fingerprint, renew at 30 days, `--force` |
| `pi/bin/ark-admin-recover` | `0700 root:root`, runs the Python as `pi` (keeps WAL owned by the service user) |
| `pi/nginx/ark-admin.conf` | `:8443` management origin (`/admin` + stylesheet only, unknown Host rejected) and `:8444` loopback-only setup listener; blanks `X-ARK-Setup-Channel` on `:8443` |
| `pi/nginx/ark.conf` | public `:80`; `location ^~ /admin { return 404; }` |
| `pi/steps/65-admin.sh` | installs the above and proves each door (404 / 403 / TLS-only, Community and Games behind the sign-in, `:8445` player origin) |
| `pi/steps/90-verify.sh` | read-only health report: every page, every listener, the admin door, the game shelf and player origin, the File Box folders, free space, the network timer + pin + last watchdog decision, the backup tools + newest copy and its age (soft) + the recovery sheet |
| `tests/` | 267 pytest tests, throwaway paths and databases, no Pi needed |

### 8.2 Ports and who may reach them

| Listener | Serves | Reachable from |
|---|---|---|
| `:80` | the whole public portal | anyone on Wi-Fi or LAN; **404 for `/admin`** |
| `:8443` | `/admin` + `/assets/ark-admin.css`, TLS only | the LAN — but a sign-in is required |
| `:8444` | `/admin` setup only, loopback-bound | only through an SSH tunnel (`ssh -N -L 8444:127.0.0.1:8444 pi@ark.local`) |
| `:8445` | `/arcade/` + `/assets/`, TLS only | the LAN — for player names and saves; `404` for `/admin` |
| `:8081` `:8083` `:8085` | kiwix / calibre / API, proxied by nginx | `127.0.0.1` only — the unit binds loopback, so the proxy is the only way in |

`http://ark.lan/recovery.txt` is not a service: it is `/srv/ark/portal/recovery.txt` served by `location /`, so it comes off `:80` beside the front page — plain text, no sign-in, installed by phase `95-backup`.

### 8.3 State on the Pi

| Path | What it is |
|---|---|
| `/srv/ark/api/data/ark.db` | accounts, sessions, audit rows, notes, files, games/revisions, player names and saves, `settings` (`public_board_post`, `public_uploads`, `arcade_players`, `arcade_auto_install`) |
| `/srv/ark/api/data/ark.installed` | marker **outside** the DB: a wiped DB ⇒ `recovery-required`, never a recreated default |
| `/srv/ark/drop/files/` | published shared files, `0644`, served as `/drop/<stored name>`; the stored name is `<16 hex>-<label>` |
| `/srv/ark/drop/tmp/` | partial uploads (`upload-*.part`), **never served**; swept at startup if older than 6 h |
| `/srv/ark/arcade/games/` | published game files, `0644`, served only through `/arcade/g/<slug>/rom`; written only by `ark_arcade_install.publish_candidate()` |
| `/srv/ark/arcade/saves/<player>/<game>/` | a named player's saves, `0700` folders and `0600` files; written only by `player_save_put()`; **never** a static folder |
| `/srv/ark/arcade/tmp/` | a download waiting to be proved (`rom-*.part`); swept at startup; never served |
| `/srv/ark/calibre-incoming/.imported.json` | import ledger keyed by sha256 — the only reason a re-drop is not a duplicate |
| `/srv/ark/calibre-incoming/done/` | incoming books already in the library; **never rescanned** |
| `/etc/ark/calibre-admin` | Calibre-Web credentials the importer uses over loopback (**not** in git) |
| `/etc/ssl/ark/ark-admin.crt` / `.key` | certificate (0644) and key (**0600 root:root**) |
| `/usr/local/sbin/ark-admin-recover` | the only way back in without a password; console only |
| `/etc/ark/mode.pinned` | the owner's network answer (`home` / `hotspot`); **absent = auto**. Written only by `ark-mode` |
| `/srv/ark/logs/net-watch.state` | the watchdog's counters: `lost_streak`, `home_streak`, `home_block_until` |
| `/srv/ark/logs/net-watch.log` | one line per decision that acted; a quiet check writes nothing |
| `/srv/ark/backup/` | the copies: `ark-backup-<date>-<time>/`, `0700 pi:pi`. A bundle holds the accounts table, so the folder is closed — and a copy that never leaves the card is not insurance |
| `/srv/ark/RECOVERY-SHEET.txt` | the printed page for a card that does not boot; the same file as `/srv/ark/portal/recovery.txt` → `http://ark.lan/recovery.txt` |

### 8.4 Commands that matter

```bash
# tests (Mac, throwaway DBs, never the live library)
python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -r tests/requirements.txt
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -o addopts='--strict-markers' -q    # prints "267 passed"
# `pytest.ini` already passes -q, so a second -q hides the summary line

# the honest answer, on the box or from the Mac
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://ark.lan/api/ready   # 200 ok, 503 = look
curl -s http://ark.lan/api/ready | python3 -m json.tool             # every part + reason
# /api/health is liveness only and says so; never quote it as a verdict

# the network, without touching the radio
sudo ark-mode status                         # mode + address + who chose it
sudo ark-net-watch --decide auto 0 0 1 0 0 2 0 0    # pure table: no link → to-hotspot
sudo ark-net-watch --dry-run                 # what it sees, what it would do
tail -n 20 /srv/ark/logs/net-watch.log       # what it actually did

# the game shelf (console only)
sudo ark-arcade --status                     # games, player names, last check
sudo ark-arcade --seed                       # re-check the pinned first shelf
sudo ark-arcade --discover --auto            # what the weekly timer runs
sudo ark-arcade --list                       # suggestions and why each was refused

# push (never force, never move a tag)
GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 GIT_SSH_COMMAND='ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=20' \
  git push origin feature/admin-hardening

# deploy / verify on the Pi (only with the owner's explicit go-ahead)
# 30-calibre ships the new importer binary and enables ark-book-import.path
# 70-network installs/enables ark-net-watch.service + .timer
sudo bash /srv/ark/src/install-ark.sh --only 20-storage,30-calibre,60-portal,65-admin,70-network,95-backup
sudo bash /srv/ark/src/install-ark.sh --only 90-verify
cat /srv/ark/ark_status.json

# copies of the records (a copy on the same card is not insurance)
sudo ark-backup --to /srv/ark/backup && sudo ark-backup --list
sudo ark-backup --verify /srv/ark/backup/ark-backup-<date>-<time>
sudo ark-restore --list                      # what could be put back

# the owner's door
ssh -N -L 8444:127.0.0.1:8444 pi@ark.local    # first claim only
sudo ark-tls-cert --show                      # compare with the browser
sudo ark-admin-recover --list                 # locked out (console only)
```

Bootstrap account: `admin`, `must_change=1`, claimable **only** through the loopback listener while unclaimed. Its one-time password is generated at first start into `/srv/ark/api/data/bootstrap-password` (`0600`) and is **not** in git; `ARK_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD` is only the test/automation hook.

### 8.5 Defects: what stages 3–6 and ARK Arcade fixed, and what is still open

Fixed on `feature/admin-hardening` (each one now has a test that fails without the fix):

* Upload names collide — replaced by one write path in `pi/api/ark_storage.py`: a 128-bit random
  stored name, `os.link` so a collision is impossible rather than unlikely, spool in
  `/srv/ark/drop/tmp` (never served), cap enforced while streaming, 512 MiB reserve, empty-file
  refusal, orphan-temp reconcile at start. The display name is only a label.
* Search "12 s" deadline drained by `shutdown()` joining every worker — one process-wide pool
  (`SEARCH_WORKERS = 6`) plus `future.result(timeout=…)` over the unfinished set only.
* nginx `4g` vs API 2 GiB — `client_max_body_size 2064m` (2 GiB + 16 MiB framing), and
  `tests/test_config.py` keeps the two numbers tied to `ark_storage.MAX_FILE`.
* `kiwix-serve` on `*:8081` — the unit now binds `127.0.0.1:8081`.
* Imports duplicated on every rescan — ledger keyed by sha256 in
  `/srv/ark/calibre-incoming/.imported.json`, `done/` never rescanned, File Box copies never moved
  (the download link survives).
* No moderation, no switch — `/admin/community`: two switches, pin/sorted-out/hide for notes,
  hide for files, delete behind a password re-auth; every action audited, hidden items stay in the
  database, reversible except delete.
* A green dot that could not be wrong, and `/api/health` answering `{"ok": true}` whatever the
  box was doing — readiness now lives in `pi/api/ark_health.py`: **ready = content present *and*
  the loopback service answered**, with a `reason` and an `action`; `/api/ready` answers 200/503;
  `/api/health` is liveness only and says so in its own body; `/admin/health` is the owner's page.
  `content` (anything on the card yet?) is what tells "still filling up" from "broken".
* Progress that was one percentage and nothing else — `ark-zim-download` now measures its own
  rate (byte delta over elapsed time) and `ark_health.progress()` reports `overall_percent`,
  `remaining_bytes`, `rate_bps`, `eta_seconds` and `stalled`. Unmeasured ⇒ `None`, never 0.
* The network was decided once, at boot — a home Wi-Fi that disappeared from under a running box
  left it unreachable until a human rebooted it. `pi/bin/ark-net-watch` now asks the same question
  every minute under `ark-net-watch.timer`: two lost checks ⇒ `ark-mode hotspot --auto`; home back
  in range, three quiet checks and **nobody on the hotspot** ⇒ home again; 30-minute back-off after
  a return that failed. It never overrules a pin (`/etc/ark/mode.pinned`) and never drops a
  connected reader — an uncountable station list (`-1`) counts as "somebody may be there".
* Free space was one number with no idea where it went — `ark_health.storage_breakdown()` measures
  five folders (`Offline Library`, `Books`, `Manuals & Guides`, `File Box`, `Settings and records`)
  plus `card_facts()` for the card total and used; **What is working** renders it with a
  plain-English summary and lists only non-empty folders. Unmeasurable ⇒ `None`, never 0.
* A backup nobody had read back — one dated bundle now has a sha256 manifest, is built as
  `.partial` and published by rename, is proved by `--verify`, and is put back only by
  `ark-restore` after it proves every byte and refuses an occupied box. The printed recovery
  sheet is the page that still works when the box does not.
* A game shelf that could trust a browser or a stored path — the player is vendored (no external
  asset), player pages live on the separate `:8445` origin, the public `:80` location blanks
  `X-ARK-Player-Origin`, player tables/cookie cannot reach `/admin`, and a stored game filename
  that is not a bare name is refused by `rom_path`, `_check_game` and the ROM route.
* **The book shelf was a management console** (R3, `docs/REQUIREMENTS-MATRIX.md`) — `/books` was
  proxied wholesale, so Calibre-Web's admin, account, upload and delete routes were reachable from
  the portal. `pi/nginx/ark.conf` now has two doors on `/books`: a 404 for every management and
  account route (named from the installed 0.6.27 `url_map`, 231 rules) and `limit_except GET`
  for the rest. Reading, search, OPDS, covers and downloads are untouched; the importer is
  unaffected because it talks to `127.0.0.1:8083` directly. `tests/test_config.py` compiles the
  shipped regex and runs it against the transcribed inventory in both directions. **Proved on
  the box** on a throwaway `:18080` listener (log §14), and the same answers were proved **on
  the live listener** by `60-portal`'s `must_be` proofs during the deploy (log §15).

**Still open, and they are deliberate limits, not oversights:**

* Public board posting and File Box uploads are **still anonymous** — the switch turns writing
  off, it never identifies who wrote. There are no per-person quotas, no quarantine and no trash.
* nginx now agrees with the app on 2 GiB, but nothing tests a real 2 GiB upload over the wire.
* Readiness looks; it cannot change. There are no pause/resume *buttons* (the `ark-zim-pause`
  file still pauses the queue) and no reason recorded for the last import. The storage breakdown
  measures the folders it knows about; it cannot change them either.
* The watchdog acts on what NetworkManager and `iw` report. If `iw` is missing or the radio is
  busy broadcasting, `ap_clients()` answers `-1` and the box deliberately stays on the hotspot
  rather than risk cutting off a reader.

Which test proves which fix (useful when a future change breaks one):

| Behaviour | Test file |
|---|---|
| unique stored names, no overwrite, empty refusal, cap, reserve, temp sweep | `tests/test_storage.py` (11) |
| pin / sorted out / hide / restore, both switches, re-auth delete, CSRF, allowlist | `tests/test_board.py` (15) |
| one import per sha256, renamed re-drop, `done/` skipped, File Box link kept | `tests/test_import.py` (7) |
| the deadline holds when one collection is slow | `tests/test_search.py` (6) |
| nginx `2064m` still equals the app's 2 GiB | `tests/test_config.py` (3) |
| schema version, migration/upgrade path, the Pi password vector | `tests/test_db.py` (7) |
| empty vs broken vs ready per section, a full card, measured progress, `/api/ready` 503, liveness-only `/api/health`, the owner page, the storage breakdown, a missing or stale copy, a copy the pages cannot open, a File Box row whose file has gone, an unreadable folder | `tests/test_health.py` (24) |
| the 14-case decision table, a fake `nmcli`/`iw` observer, pins, back-off, the timer/unit files, wiring to `ark-mode` | `tests/test_network.py` (28) |
| a bundle round trip, a damaged copy refused, `.partial` swept, rotation, `--force` on an occupied box, a shared file never replaced, a path escape refused, a named player's save carried, the sheet's plain words, a root-made bundle handed to the box (and not `chown`ed when run as `pi`) | `tests/test_backup.py` (17) |
| guest vs named saves, origin header, hidden game, export/import, owner Games page, hide/show, suggestions | `tests/test_arcade.py` (26) |
| licence recorded not refused, language table (English / wordless / unrecorded), a GBA entry fetched from its own repository, per-console allowlist, redirect, archive, file shape, seed, a carried shelf installed from a folder, discovery, auto-install switch | `tests/test_arcade_install.py` (35) |

### 8.6 Delivered and not built yet

Stage 3 delivered: upload safety (unique stored names, one write path, cap + reserve, empty-file
refusal, temp reconcile), import idempotency (sha256 ledger, `done/` exclusion, preserved File Box
links), board moderation + the two Community switches, and bounded search.

Stage 4 delivered: one readiness module (`pi/api/ark_health.py`) that names the reason, `/api/ready`,
`/admin/health`, `/api/health` reduced to liveness, and measured download progress (rate, bytes left,
ETA, stalled).

Stage 5 delivered: `pi/bin/ark-net-watch` + `ark-net-watch.service`/`.timer` (the network is asked
about every minute after boot, not only at boot), `ark-mode` pins (`home`/`hotspot` pin, `auto`
unpins, `hotspot --auto` leaves none), phase `70-network` installing and proving all of it,
`90-verify` reporting the timer, the pin and the last decision, and a measured storage breakdown
with card facts on **What is working** and in `/api/status`.

Stage 6 delivered: `pi/bin/ark-backup` / `pi/bin/ark-restore` on one shared definition
(`pi/bin/ark_bundle.py`), `pi/RECOVERY-SHEET.txt` served as `http://ark.lan/recovery.txt`, phase
`95-backup`, a **Latest copy** readiness row (`ark_health.backup_section()`, never blocking), the
verifier reporting copies and their age, and `tests/test_backup.py` (13 at that checkpoint; 14 at
the tip). **Deployed 25 Oct 2026 (§8.12a)**; one bundle written on the box and verified whole, no
restore that replaces records yet: §8.10.

ARK Arcade delivered after stage 6: the public shelf `http://ark.lan/arcade/`, a vendored
EmulatorJS player, optional named-player saves on the separate `:8445` TLS origin, the owner
**Games** page, schema v3 tables, `ark-arcade-discover.timer`, and named players' saves carried by
`ark-backup`/`ark-restore`. Tests: `tests/test_arcade.py` (26), `tests/test_arcade_install.py`
(35), and one backup save case. **Deployed 25 Oct 2026 (§8.12a)**; since 25 Oct the shelf
holds **23 curated games** (10 GB, 6 GBC, 6 GBA, 1 NES), fetched from the per-console
catalogues and served — but **unplayed on the box**: no game has been played there yet (§8.11).

Still absent, **do not describe any of it as done**: emergency content review; essentials pack, QR
cards, accessibility and the household page (stage 7); per-person quotas; quarantine/approval
queue; trash; pause/resume buttons on the owner page; a backup timer, or any backup button on the
owner page; player password recovery, per-player save quota, save trash, or an owner reset/delete
for a player name; a **Check now** button for games (the weekly timer is the only check).
**Never invent a control that does not exist.**

Two page-size facts, so nobody documents them wrongly again:

* the public board **does** paginate — `BOARD_PAGE = 20`, with Older/Newer links and a total;
* the owner's Community page **does not** — it shows the newest 50 notes and the newest 50 files
  (`LIMIT 50`) and says so in its own headings. That is the limit to state, not "the last fifty
  items of the board".

**Never quote `/api/health` as evidence the box is well.** It answers `{"ok": true}` whenever the web process is up, and its own body now says `scope: "backend-process"` and points at `/api/ready` — quoting it as a verdict is the mistake that made stage 4 necessary. `/api/ready` is the honest one (200 = every part that must work does, 503 = look at `blocking`), and `/api/status` carries the same per-section `ready`/`reason` for the old page.

### 8.7 Gotchas that have already cost time

* **`run_commands` batches run in parallel.** A `pkill` or restart races the probe meant to observe it; two servers binding one port in the same batch fail in a way that looks like a code bug. Sequence state-changers first, observers second.
* **Apostrophes in commit subjects break zsh quoting** — git then reads the message as pathspecs. Write the message to a file and use `git commit -F /tmp/msg.txt`.
* **Tests need peer trust:** a hand-rolled `TestClient` sees peer `testclient`; set `ARK_TRUSTED_PROXIES=testclient,127.0.0.1,::1` (the fixtures already do) or every admin request is a correct 403.
* **`ark_admin` needs `ensure_initialised()`** (the real app does it at import); a bare router mount on an empty directory raises `no such table: admins`.
* **macOS Python 3.9 links LibreSSL**, so stdlib `hashlib.scrypt` is absent — `cryptography` is the fallback. The Pi vector pinned in `tests/test_db.py` proves the two agree.
* Reading must survive a broken admin store: the `CRYPTO_ERROR` path keeps the public portal up.
* **`pytest -q` twice prints no summary.** `pytest.ini` already passes `-q`, so `-q -q` shows dots and no "267 passed" line; use `-o addopts='--strict-markers' -q` when the count is the evidence.
* **macOS has no `timeout` command** (`zsh: command not found: timeout`, coreutils not installed). Bound a command with `gtimeout` if present, or start it in the background with a log file and poll — do not build a chain that depends on `timeout`.
* **`git push` from a non-interactive session can hang on an SSH prompt.** Use `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 GIT_SSH_COMMAND='ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=20'`; a failed push then reports instead of waiting for a passphrase.
* **SQLite `ADD COLUMN` cannot add `NOT NULL` without a `DEFAULT`.** Schema v2 relies on `DEFAULT ''` / `DEFAULT 0`; that is why an upgraded box has `files.stored = ''` for old rows and every read goes through `_stored_name()`. A new query that reads `stored` directly will produce empty download links on an upgraded card.
* **The upload guard is one function, not a convention.** The cap, the 512 MiB reserve and the empty-file refusal all live inside `ark_storage.spool()`. Replacing `file.file.read` with `shutil.copyfileobj` anywhere would silently drop all three — `tests/test_storage.py` covers `spool()`, not the route, so the route must keep calling it.
* **A timed-out search worker is not stopped.** `_kiwix_one` waits 9 s while `SEARCH_DEADLINE` is 6 s, on purpose; the page returns at 6 s and the late answer is discarded, but that thread still occupies one of the 6 pool slots. Do not shrink `SEARCH_WORKERS` without measuring repeated searches.
* **Readiness has exactly one author.** `pi/api/ark_health.py` decides; `main.py` and `ark_admin.py` only render it. A new "is it ready?" test written anywhere else will disagree with the owner page within a week.
* **`ready` and `content` are different questions.** `content` = is anything on the card yet (`no` means "still filling up", amber); `ready` = content exists **and** the loopback service answered. Collapsing them makes an empty box look broken or a broken box look fine — the exact defect stage 4 removed.
* **Any HTTP status counts as "answering".** A reader that returns 500 is up; a reader whose port refuses is down. `answers()` treats a response as life on purpose, so do not "improve" it into a 200-only check.
* **Probes are cached for 10 s** (`forget_probes()` clears the cache; the owner page calls it). A test that flips a stub server on or off must clear the cache, or it asserts against the previous answer.
* **`ark_health` paths are read at call time** from `ARK_ROOT` / `ZIM_DIR`. Binding a path to a module constant at import time is how `main.py` used to freeze a directory before the tests could point it somewhere else.
* **Downloads never block readiness.** `BLOCKING_SECTIONS` excludes `downloads`; a stalled queue is reported (`stalled: true`) and still leaves the box usable. Do not add it to the blocking set.
* **`ark-zim-download` is the only authority on rate.** It writes the measured byte delta; `progress()` reads it and returns `None` when it was not measured. Never compute an ETA from an assumed speed.
* **A readiness test needs a real listener.** `tests/test_health.py` starts `http.server` on a loopback port so "answering" and "refused" are two states; mocking the socket layer instead would test the mock.
* **`KIWIX_INTERNAL` is a unit setting; `CALIBRE_INTERNAL` is only a code default.** `pi/systemd/ark-api.service` sets the library address explicitly but not the shelf one, so the bookshelf probe reads the built-in `127.0.0.1:8083`. If calibre ever moves port, `ark_health` keeps probing the old one and the owner page says "not answering" while the shelf is fine. Set both in the unit before changing a port; the probe address should never be an implicit default.
* **A number that may be unknown must render as "not measured yet", never as `0`.** `progress()` returns `None` for rate, ETA and bytes-left when nobody measured them, and the front-page strip therefore omits those figures rather than printing zero (`index.html`: `if (dl.eta_seconds)`). `0 %` and `0 B/s` are claims — write them only when they are true. The owner's *What is working* page goes further and prints only the reason, no percentages at all; do not add a figure to it that the box cannot measure.
* **The network decision is a pure shell function on purpose.** `decide()` in `pi/bin/ark-net-watch` takes nine numbers and prints `stay`/`to-hotspot`/`to-home`; `--decide` exposes it with no radio, no root and no NetworkManager. `tests/test_network.py` drives that table directly (28 collected tests, the 14-case table is parametrized) and separately stubs `nmcli`/`iw` against the observer. Move the table into a "cleaner" place and the test stops testing the shipped script.
* **A station count it could not read is `-1`, not `0`.** `iw` may be absent or the radio busy broadcasting; `ap_clients()` then answers `-1` and `decide()` treats anything non-zero — including `-1` — as "somebody may be there". A `0` there would drop a reader mid-page. Never "fix" `-1` into a zero.
* **A pin suppresses only the return home, never the rescue.** `ark-mode home` / `ark-mode hotspot` write `/etc/ark/mode.pinned`; `ark-mode auto` removes it; the watchdog's own rescue calls `ark-mode hotspot --auto`, which deliberately leaves none — that is how "the box did this" stays distinguishable from "the owner said so". A pin must never be able to leave the box dark.
* **The watchdog acts only through `ark-mode`.** It reads NetworkManager and `iw` and writes a log line and its state file, nothing else; `ARK_MODE_BIN` is the seam the tests stub. Bringing NetworkManager up directly from the watcher would create a second writer to the network, and the two would disagree within a week.
* **`net-watch.log` is quiet by design.** Only decisions that *did* something are logged; a short log is a healthy day, and "the log is nearly empty" is not evidence the timer is dead — `systemctl status ark-net-watch.timer` and `ark-net-watch --dry-run` are.
* **A row and its file are two different facts.** `files` rows and the folder `drop/files` can disagree, and `ark-restore --no-files` is a *supported* way to create exactly that (records back, File Box empty). The download route gates on the row **and** the file; the listing gates on the file too (`main._present()`, which also corrects the number in the heading); the owner's **Shared files** page keeps showing the row so a gone file can be deleted, and its audit line says *“record removed; the file was already gone”*. Never 'tidy' this by deleting rows or files automatically — that is data loss wearing a maintenance costume.
* **A copy's `counts` come from the database, not from the folder.** `manifest.json` records `counts` read from the **copied database** (`SELECT COUNT(*) FROM notes` / `files` / `admins`), so a file dropped into `drop/files` by hand (no `files` row) is copied but not counted — "0 shared files" beside a bundle that holds one is honest bookkeeping, not a bug. A real upload always has a row: `ark_storage.spool()` writes the file and its row together.
* **`--list` answers for the default folder, not the last `--to`.** It lists `ARK_BACKUP_DIR` (`<ARK_ROOT>/backup` on the box); asking about somewhere else is `--to <that folder> --list`.
* **`ark-backup` writes its report to stdout only.** `say()` is stdout, refusals are `Not done: …` on stderr, so `95-backup.sh`'s `head -n 1` parse of the dry run is safe — keep any new progress noise off stdout.
* **A restore is the one deliberate writer of the records.** It verifies every byte first, refuses an occupied box without `--force`, never replaces a shared file already there, parks the import list as `.imported.json.from-backup` when the shelf is missing, and sweeps `.partial` folders rather than listing them. Never teach anything else to "just copy the newest folder" — §6 still names `ark_storage.spool()` as the only writer of `drop/files`.
* **An age the box cannot read is prose, never `0`.** `_age_hours()` uses the manifest's `created`, falls back to the folder's mtime, then to `None` → "at a time the box could not read". `COPY_STALE_HOURS = 168` only makes the row *not ready*; `backup` is not in `BLOCKING_SECTIONS`, so a missing copy can never make a serving box look broken (§8.8 rule 11).
* **A licence is information, and that is a code fact, not a policy note.** `ark_arcade.license_problem()` returns `""` for every input and `ark_arcade_install.rights_facts()` always answers `(True, "")` — both kept so the pages and the tests share one name. Do not reintroduce a refusal for NC/ND, "freeware", "all rights reserved" or a blank licence, and do not re-add a rights-evidence requirement: the owner decided on 25 Oct 2026 that the shelf is built from what is good to play, and the licence text is shown on the game's card as provenance.
* **Language is the only remaining refusal, and only when the source says so.** `language_facts()` refuses a *recorded* non-English language and a recorded conflict; an unrecorded language is allowed, because most homebrew entries say nothing. A test that insists "unrecorded ⇒ refused" is testing the defect that kept 38 of 40 candidates off the shelf.
* **Each console has its own repository, and the prefix is chosen by the resolved system.** GB/GBC → `gbdev/database`, GBA → `gbadev-org/games`, NES → `nesdev-org/homebrew-db` (`SOURCE_RAW_BY_SYSTEM`). A single prefix 404'd every GBA and NES candidate for a day. SNES/Genesis map to `""` — no repository, so `entry_to_candidate()` returns `None` rather than inventing an address.
* **A search answer may omit `platform`.** `_system()` falls back to the extension when the entry names no console (`_EXT_SYSTEM`; `.bin` is deliberately absent because two consoles share it). Without that fallback a perfectly good `.gb` entry was dropped as "cannot be named".
* **`ark-arcade --seed --from-dir` exists because the box may have no internet.** It publishes the pinned shelf from carried files through the same `publish_candidate(..., source=…)` path, so the recorded SHA-256 and size are still enforced: a different file with the right name is refused, and re-running skips what is already there. Do not teach the installer to `cp` into `games/` — that would bypass every check.
* **`ark.lan` is not a name a Mac can look up.** It resolves only while the box is broadcasting (its own dnsmasq answers every name) or on a network whose DNS knows it; `.local` is the only special-use name mDNS answers. So the owner's door is `https://ark.local:8443/admin` on a home LAN and `https://ark.lan:8443/admin` from a phone on the hotspot. `https://<lan-ip>:8443` sends no SNI, hits the management listener's default server and is refused on purpose — that is not a broken door, and the certificate already covers both names.
* **`boot --dry-run` is how a cold boot is proved without rebooting.** `ark-mode boot --dry-run` prints the decision from `BOOT_MODE` and touches nothing (it does not even need root). `BOOT_MODE=hotspot` is the shipped default and it *pins*, so the watchdog will not walk the box back onto a router nobody asked for; `ark-mode auto` releases it. Never "prove" the boot path by switching the radio under an SSH session.

* **An owner-supplied game carries its own fingerprint now; do not remove it.** `owner_candidate()` records `arc.sha256_file(source)`, which is what lets `publish_candidate()` answer *"already on the shelf, unchanged"* and what lets `ark_arcade.game_by_sha()` refuse the same bytes under a second name. Without it the dedup branch is dead code for owner files — that defect put `stars-r2.gb` on the card for a file that had not changed. `--dry-run` computes it too, so a dry run reads the whole file (≤ 64 MB) and proves the path, the extension, the size and the language — **not** the console header, which is only checked inside `publish_candidate()`.
* **`ark-arcade` is run as `pi`, never with `sudo`.** `/usr/local/sbin` is not on `pi`'s `PATH` (that is why `docs/ADMIN.md` shows the full path), and it does not need root: `/srv/ark/arcade/games` and `/srv/ark/api/data` are `pi:pi`, and both services that also write them run as `pi`. A root-run import leaves root-owned game files and root-written rows — the same fault as the root-made backup bundle the owner page could not read (`copies_unreadable()`). There is **no drop folder and no upload button**: the File Box does not feed the shelf, a zip is not unpacked, and nothing watches a game folder.
* **An owner's words reach the game page, so escape them.** `--description` is the line under the title (it used to be *"Ready to play."* for every hand-added game) and `--notices` is the credits line under the facts — both rendered through `arc.esc()`, because a person typed them. `notices` was stored for a month without ever being shown; if a field like it is added to the shelf, render it or stop offering the flag.
* **`ark_db.tx(conn)` is nested-aware now; do not open a second writer around it.** The shelf publishes a save row and a file together, so `tx()` accepts a connection the caller already holds and turns into a no-op when that connection is in a transaction. Before this, `with ark_db.tx() as conn: ... arcade_save(...)` opened a second `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` and deadlocked with `database is locked`. The `tx(conn)` call shape still works; `tx(immediate=False)` still works.
* **The arcade's app files and the card's data are two different folders.** `src_root()` (`ARK_ARCADE_SRC`, `/srv/ark/src/arcade`) holds templates, CSS, seed list and the vendored player; `data_root()` (`ARK_ARCADE_DATA`, `<ARK_ROOT>/arcade`) holds `games/`, `saves/` and `tmp/`. Binding one to the other puts the player in the writable data folder or the saves in a read-only source tree.
* **`X-ARK-Player-Origin` is a trust boundary, not a hint.** `:8445` sets it to `1`; the public `:80` location sets it to empty, because nginx would otherwise pass a visitor's invented header through. `ark_arcade_web._player_origin()` is the only reader; a new route that returns a save or sets a player cookie must call it, or it will do so through the public portal.
* **Python 3.9 is still the floor for `pi/`.** An f-string cannot contain a backslash in its expression part; `f"{json.dumps(v).replace('</', '<\\/')}"` is a `SyntaxError` on the Mac's Python and on the box. Precompute it (`_js()` in `ark_arcade_web.py`) and test with `python3 -m py_compile`.
* **A player cookie is `Secure`.** It is issued only on `:8445` and deliberately does not travel over plain HTTP; the public shelf therefore shows a signed-in reader as a guest and sends **My saves** to `https://<host>:8445/arcade/player`. A public page must never render the save panel merely because a cookie was present.

* **A row can outlive its file, on purpose — so the pages filter, never the database.** `ark-restore --no-files` puts the records back on a card whose File Box is empty, and a folder tidied by hand leaves rows behind; both are supported, not corrupt. `ark_storage.present_names()` is the one reader of "what is really in the folder", the listing (`main._present()`) and the owner page (`ark_health.files_section()`) both count only what a reader can open, and **neither deletes anything** — deleting a record or a file to tidy a listing is data loss wearing a maintenance costume. The owner's **Shared files** list is where a leftover is seen and removed, with an audit line that already says *"the file was already gone"*.
* **`present_names()` answers `None`, not an empty set, when the folder cannot be listed.** An unreadable folder is not an empty box: the listing shows the rows unfiltered and `files_section()` reports `files_missing: None`. A `0` there tells the owner nothing was ever shared.
* **`sudo ark-backup` writes as root, and the pages that report copies run as `pi`.** A `root:root 0700` bundle inside the `pi:pi 0700` copy folder is unreadable to `ark_health`, which then said "no copy has been made yet" beside a copy that was sitting right there. `ark-backup` now gives each published bundle (and its `.lock`) the **destination folder's owner** (`_give_to_owner()`/`_adopt()`, root only, never fatal), phase `95-backup` adopts bundles that already exist, and `copies_unreadable()` makes a copy nobody can open a sentence of its own — never "no copy" (§8.7's `None` rule, one folder over). `ark-restore` already had the same idea, chowning restored files to the `pi` account unless `--keep-owner`.
* **A TLS probe must ask by NAME.** A request to `https://127.0.0.1:8443/…` carries no SNI, so nginx serves the default server — and the management listener's default server answers `ssl_reject_handshake on`, so the connection dies and curl reports `000` on a healthy door. That failed phase `65-admin`'s own proof on the box on 25 Oct 2026. Use `ark_tls_code <host:port> <path>` from `pi/lib/common.sh` (it adds `-k --resolve "<host>:127.0.0.1"`); never write a TLS probe of your own in a step — the first fix went into `65-admin` only and `90-verify` then failed twenty minutes later with the same `000`. Plain-HTTP probes are the opposite and must keep the address literal: they are asking the port, not the name.
* **`bash -n` cannot see a doubled quote.** `x="$(cmd)""` leaves a string open; if any later line closes it the file stays "balanced", `bash -n` passes, and the assignment swallows whole lines — the script then dies at runtime with something like `adm_code: unbound variable`. That is exactly how a patch of mine broke `90-verify` on 25 Oct 2026. `tests/test_tls_probe.py` now fails if any line of the three shell files ends with `)""`. When Bash "passes" but a variable is unbound, look for a stray quote before looking for a logic bug.
* **The book door is a named route list, and a GET rule cannot replace it.** `limit_except GET` in front of Calibre-Web refuses every POST/PUT/DELETE, but `/admin/*`, `/login`, `/me`, `/generate_auth_token/<id>`, `/shelf/create` and the admin-only `/ajax` endpoints are reachable with GET alone — they are named individually in the 404 door in `pi/nginx/ark.conf`. Do not "simplify" them into `location ^~ /books { return 404; }` or drop one of them: `tests/test_config.py` compiles that exact regex out of the shipped file and runs the transcribed 0.6.27 inventory through it in both directions. `/books/ajax/listbooks` and `/books/ajax/book/<uuid>` are deliberately left open (the table view pages with them) and `/books/advsearch` is the single POST that only reads. When Calibre-Web is upgraded, re-enumerate `calibreweb/cps/*.py` and change the config, the test and `docs/REQUIREMENTS-MATRIX.md` in one commit; when the shelf page starts using a new route, add it to `READING` and to the installer's `must_be` proofs.
* **The player needs a box with a height, and that box must be OURS.** `#arcade-frame` carries the aspect ratio and `#arcade-stage` fills it. EmulatorJS writes `--ejs-primary-color` into **its own element's `style` attribute**, erasing anything inline we put there; and with no height anywhere the canvas is `height:100%` of nothing, collapses, and the game runs perfectly and invisibly — `getState()` still returns 35 KB, no error, no message, no console warning. That is what "the player does not load" was on 25 Oct 2026. Guards: `tests/test_config.py::test_the_player_box_has_a_height_to_fill`, `tests/test_arcade.py::test_the_player_is_sized_on_a_frame_the_emulator_cannot_overwrite`. Do not "simplify" the frame away.
* **A file route must answer `HEAD`.** FastAPI's `@router.get` answers HEAD with **405 `Allow: GET`** — Starlette's automatic HEAD does not apply to `APIRoute`. EmulatorJS asks for a game's size with a HEAD *before* it downloads, so every file-serving route here is `@router.api_route(..., methods=["GET", "HEAD"])`: `/arcade/g/<slug>/rom`, `/arcade/emulator/<path>`, `/arcade/static/<name>`, `/arcade/notices.txt`, `/drop/<name>`. A test proves HEAD is 200 with the right `content-length` and an empty body.
* **The library catalogue must be built beside the ZIMs it describes.** `kiwix-manage` records each path relative to the folder holding the library file: built in `/tmp` and moved to `/srv/ark/kiwix`, every path read `/srv/ark/srv/ark/kiwix/…`, so the viewer answered 200, OPDS listed every book, and search answered `<error>Content not found</error>` for every handle — for a day, with nothing logged. `pi/bin/ark-kiwix-library` now builds `mktemp "$ZIM_DIR/.library-XXXXXX"` and publishes by rename. It runs as **root** (downloader) and as **pi** (service), so it hands the file to the folder's owner; a `flock` file in that folder took the whole library down with `library.xml.lock: Permission denied` on the first attempt. Guard: `tests/test_config.py::test_the_library_catalogue_is_built_beside_the_bundles_it_describes`.
* **A collection's search handle is its ZIM's file name without `.zim`.** `…_en_all_2026-10` → 25 items; the `id` (a UUID) → 0; the `name` attribute (`…_en_all`, no date) → 0, all of them silently. `ark_health.library_books()` carries `stem` (from `path`, never from `name`) and `main._handle()` prefers it. And the endpoint is **`/kiwix/search`**, not `/search` — the bare path answers kiwix's own 404 page, whose body says "Content not found" and is indistinguishable from a failed lookup.
* **An HTTP 200 is not evidence that a page works.** Both 25 Oct defects were 200 everywhere: every asset, every route, `90-verify` green, `/api/ready` 200. What found them was a headless browser (`/tmp/pwbrowser` + `playwright`) driving the real page over `http://ark.local` and reading the emulator's own `getState()`/`getSaveFile()` — which showed a running core and a black screen at the same time. When a page "does not work" but the network is clean, ask the page, not `curl`.


### 8.8 Rules for the next stage

1. Test first, then code, then `pytest` green, then a checkpoint line in `docs/IMPLEMENTATION-LOG.md`.
2. The public reading path must keep working at every commit.
3. No deploy, reboot, `pkill`, or service restart on the live box without explicit permission.
4. Docs change in the same commit as the behaviour — `README.md`, `docs/ADMIN.md`, `docs/USING-THE-ARK.md`, `docs/REBUILD-ME-ARK.txt`, `docs/REBUILD-FROM-BACKUP.txt`, `pi/RECOVERY-SHEET.txt` and `docs/REQUIREMENTS-MATRIX.md` (the per-requirement verdict, whose every row cites the test that proves it) all describe the box and have drifted before.
5. Shared files get written by `ark_storage.spool()` and by nothing else (see §6).
6. New schema steps are a numbered migration in `ark_db.py`, bump `SCHEMA_VERSION`, never rewrite v1, and keep old rows readable (see the `stored = ''` rule in §8.7).
7. The two switches are settings, not code paths: `public_board_post` and `public_uploads`, absent or `1` means **open**. A fresh database must therefore start with the box usable, and a test must prove the closed state still serves everything already written.
8. A probe is cheap, cached and cannot raise. Anything that decides readiness goes in `ark_health` with a timeout, and no reader's page may wait on a service that is down — stage 4 exists because readiness was cheap enough to be wrong, not because it was too slow to check.
9. When a rendered value disappears, say so in the same commit: `/api/health` no longer carries `ok`, so anything outside this repo that polls it must be told to read `/api/ready` (200/503) instead.
10. The network is decided in exactly one place after boot: `pi/bin/ark-net-watch`. It may only act through `ark-mode`, it may not overrule a pin, and it may never take the hotspot away while a device is connected (an uncountable station list counts as connected). Prove any change to it with `--decide`, which needs no radio — never by experiment on the live box.
11. New measurements follow the `None` rule: a folder size, a rate or an ETA the box could not measure renders as "not measured yet", never as `0` (§8.7).
12. A copy only reads. `ark-backup` writes into its own bundle and nowhere else; `ark-restore` is the one deliberate writer of the records and of `drop/files`, and only after proving every byte (§6). No timer takes a copy without an explicit yes, no page pretends to have a backup button, and any wording about copies says out loud that a copy left on the same card does not survive that card dying.
13. The arcade's three write paths are closed sets: `ark_storage.spool()` for `drop/files`, `ark_arcade_install.publish_candidate()` for `games/`, and `player_save_put()` for `saves/`. `:8445` sets `X-ARK-Player-Origin: 1`; the public `:80` location blanks it. A game page must never contain an `http://` or `https://` asset reference, and a stored game filename must be a bare name before it is joined to a path.

### 8.9 OUTSTANDING — what is not done yet, in the order it matters

**Blocking a release (nothing is wrong with the code; it is unproven in a human's hands):**

0. **~~Deploy~~ — DONE 25 Oct 2026**, green, see §8.12a. Everything below was
   deployed with `--only 20-storage,30-calibre,60-portal,65-admin,70-network,95-backup`
   then `90-verify`; `/api/ready` answers 200; the database is at layout **v4** and
   the only way back is `/root/ark-snapshot-20261025-092106`. Re-running the same
   command is idempotent, and **the same `.backup`-first rule applies** to any
   future migration:

1. **~~Deploy stages 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6 + ARK Arcade and the 25 Oct hardening~~ — DONE 25 Oct 2026** (`--only 20-storage,30-calibre,60-portal,65-admin,70-network,95-backup`, then `90-verify`; both `ARK_INSTALL_COMPLETE`). Kept for the map of which phase owns what: 30-calibre is where the new importer binary and `ark-book-import.path` live; 60-portal carries `ark_health.py`, `/api/ready`, the storage breakdown, the arcade modules/player/first shelf and `ark-arcade-discover.timer`; 65-admin installs the `:8445` player origin as well as `:8443`; 70-network installs and enables `ark-net-watch.service` + `.timer`; 95-backup installs `ark-backup`/`ark-restore` and the recovery sheet. Then `--only 90-verify`, then read `/srv/ark/ark_status.json` and `curl -s http://ark.local/api/ready`. **A future migration still takes the database aside first**, because `60-portal.sh` restarts `ark-api`, which runs every migration in place and has no downgrade path:

   ```bash
   sudo sqlite3 /srv/ark/api/data/ark.db ".backup '/root/ark-db-before-v4.db'"
   ```

   Use `.backup`, not `cp` — the live database is WAL (`PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL`), so a plain copy can miss committed data.
2. **Walk through it on a phone** — claim through the tunnel, open **What is working** and read one reason aloud (it must be plain English, not a service name), stop `ark-kiwix` on purpose and watch that row turn to *Not ready* with a fix, post a note, upload two files with the same name, drop an `.epub` in the File Box and watch it reach the shelf without a command, try a file over 2 GiB, hide/restore, password-confirmed delete, switch both Community switches off and confirm reading and downloads still work, then switch them back on, read the storage breakdown aloud and check it matches `du -sh`, then **play a game with the internet unplugged**, make a player name, save/export/import on a second browser, and confirm `:8445` serves the same certificate as `:8443`; then **pull the home Wi-Fi out from under it** and watch the hotspot come up by itself within two minutes (and go back once the router returns and nobody is connected), then take a copy (`sudo ark-backup --to /srv/ark/backup`), verify it, and open `http://ark.lan/recovery.txt` on the phone and print it. None of this has been done by a human yet.
3. **A real 2 GiB upload over the wire** — nginx and the app now agree on the number, but no test or human has pushed a file that size through the proxy.

**Done on 25 Oct 2026 after the first deploy (see §17 of `docs/IMPLEMENTATION-LOG.md`):**

* Redeployed `--only 60-portal,70-network` from the branch tip: licence-as-information,
  per-console catalogues, `--from-dir`, `ark-mode boot` and `BOOT_MODE=hotspot`. Both runs
  `ARK_INSTALL_COMPLETE`; rollback points `/root/ark-db-before-games.db` and
  `/root/ark-src-before-games.tgz`.
* **23 games installed and serving** (`/arcade/` 200, a game page 200, its ROM 4,194,304 B
  for Apotris, no `http(s)://` asset in the page). `90-verify` green, `/api/ready` **200**,
  `ready 8 / total 8`, `blocking: []`, 121.5 GB free.
* The leftover `e2e-drop-test.epub` record (file already gone) was removed through the
