  read every fingerprint back and `--list` printed it; `ark-restore --dry-run`
  refused the occupied box. A restore that actually replaces records: still never.

**Never executed, not even once — the blind spots that remain:**

* The amber/red dot on the front page and the "What to do:" line on **What is working** have never been seen in a browser, on any device.
* `ark-zim-download`'s measured rate has never come from a real download — every figure the tests assert was a hand-written status file.
* `/api/health` has lost its `ok` field on purpose. If anything outside this repo polls it (a phone monitor, a script on the Mac), repoint it at `/api/ready` — the only endpoint whose status code is a verdict.
* `ark-net-watch` has only ever met a fake `nmcli` and a fake `iw` (`tests/test_network.py`). It has never seen a real NetworkManager scan, a real AP-mode station dump, or `iw` refusing while broadcasting — the three cases the `-1` rule exists for.
* The watchdog's real transitions — link lost → hotspot; home back with nobody on the hotspot → home; a return that failed → 30-minute back-off — have never happened on hardware once. `--dry-run` has been run on the live box; acting has not.
* The storage breakdown has been seen through `/api/status` on the box and printed by the verifier, but nobody has compared a row against `du -sh` on a full card.
* `/srv/ark/RECOVERY-SHEET.txt` and `http://ark.local/recovery.txt` have never been opened by a human, and no bundle has been copied off the card.
* The arcade's hardware path is still unplayed **by a person**: EmulatorJS has never run in a browser *on a phone or on the Pi's own hardware*, no game has been fetched by the box from the live source (**the Pi has no route to the internet** — the library must be carried over), `ark-arcade-discover.timer` has never fired there, and no phone has signed in or kept a save. What *is* proved (§18.2): the deployed pages served a Game Boy game and a Game Boy Advance game to a headless Chromium on the Mac, drew both screens, and returned real save states — so the bytes, the cores and the layout are right, and what is left is a human's thumb. `:8445` has answered over TLS with SNI and refused `/admin`; its certificate has never been compared with `:8443`'s in a browser. The bare-filename guards have only been exercised by the test suite, not by a malicious row on a real database.
* The v4 migration has run once, on the live database, never on a card that already held v4 rows; the bootstrap-password file and the constant-work sign-in have met one probe, not a person.

**Before handing the box, or its backups, to anyone:** the set in `~/Downloads/Project-Ark` was built from **tag `v1.0.5`**, so it contains none of stages 2–6 or ARK Arcade. Tag first, then regenerate it by the section 5 procedure in `docs/REBUILD-FROM-BACKUP.txt` — and keep the folder's "30 traps" line in step with `docs/REBUILD-ME-ARK.txt`.

**Owner decisions, still open:** rotate the GitHub password, rotate the Pi password, choose a licence.

**Known gaps left on purpose (say so, never fake them):** per-person quotas; a quarantine/approval step; a trash instead of for-ever delete; pagination on the Community page; pause/resume buttons on the owner page; nothing measures `/api/ready` under load, and the watchdog has no manual "switch now" button beyond `ark-mode`; no player password recovery, save quota, save trash, or owner reset/delete for a player name; no "check now" button for games. The storage row judges free space, and the breakdown measures the folders it knows — neither probes a service, and neither can delete anything.

**After the phone walk-through, stage 7** — the household layer (emergency content review, essentials pack, QR cards, accessibility, the household page). Stage 6 is delivered in §8.10 and ARK Arcade in §8.11; both are **deployed but unplayed and unseen in a browser**. Tag a release only after items 2–3 above pass; never move `v1.0.5`.

### 8.10 STAGE 6 — delivered 24 Oct 2026, **deployed 25 Oct 2026**

**Deployed on the box on 25 Oct 2026** (see §8.12a): one bundle has been written
there (`ark-backup-20261025-093643`) and `--verify` read every fingerprint back,
and `ark-restore --dry-run` refused the occupied box without touching it. Still
unproven: no restore has ever replaced records, and `/recovery.txt` has never
been opened in a browser. At this checkpoint `pytest` was **144 passed**
(12 files); it stood at **174** after ARK Arcade and is **267** now. Detail and
the honest gaps: `docs/IMPLEMENTATION-LOG.md` §11 and §15; the per-requirement
verdict is `docs/REQUIREMENTS-MATRIX.md`.

The code, the tests and every doc in §8.8 rule 4 are **committed and pushed** on
`feature/admin-hardening`; run `git status --short` first, but do not expect
stage 6 to be missing from `origin` any more.

| Piece | What it does |
|---|---|
| `pi/bin/ark_bundle.py` | what a bundle **is**, defined once: layout, manifest format 1 JSON, `safe_rel()` / `inside()` path rules, sha256 per file, `verify_bundle()`, `copy_database()` with SQLite's own `backup()` then `PRAGMA journal_mode=DELETE` (never `cp` — WAL). Both tools import it |
| `pi/bin/ark-backup` | `--to` (default `/srv/ark/backup`), `--keep 2`, `--no-files`, `--dry-run`, `--list`, `--verify`, `--quiet`. Builds `<name>.partial`, manifest last, publishes by rename, sweeps stale `.partial`, `flock`s the destination, records counts + `SCHEMA_VERSION` + `git describe`, writes `RESTORE-ME.txt` inside every bundle. It only reads |
| `pi/bin/ark-restore` | `--from`, `--list`, `--dry-run`, `--force`, `--no-files`, `--ledger auto\|yes\|no`, `--keep-owner`. Verifies every byte **before** touching the box; refuses an occupied box without `--force`; never replaces a shared file already there; leaves the import list beside an empty shelf as `.imported.json.from-backup`; refuses any path that climbs out of the bundle or the box |
| `pi/RECOVERY-SHEET.txt` | the printed page for the day the card does not boot — phone + card reader + working card, plain words only. Symlinked at `docs/RECOVERY-SHEET.txt`; installed to `/srv/ark/RECOVERY-SHEET.txt` and `/srv/ark/portal/recovery.txt` (`http://ark.lan/recovery.txt`) |
| `pi/steps/95-backup.sh` | phase 8: install both tools (`ark_bundle.py` beside them), create `/srv/ark/backup` `0700 pi:pi`, install the sheet, prove `--dry-run`, print the four commands. **No timer, on purpose** |
| `ark_health.backup_section()` | **Latest copy** row on **What is working** and in `/api/status`: finished bundles only (a `.partial` is not one), newest age + what it holds, *Not ready* only when there is none or it is older than `COPY_STALE_HOURS = 168`; **not** in `BLOCKING_SECTIONS` — a missing copy never makes a serving box look broken |
| `pi/steps/90-verify.sh` | reports both tools, the newest copy and its age (soft, never a failure), how many are kept, the sheet's presence; `ark_status.json` gains `"backup": {copies, newest, made}` |
| `tests/test_backup.py` | 13 tests at this checkpoint (14 now, with the named-player save case): round trip, damaged copy refused, `.partial` swept and never listed, `--keep` rotation, occupied box needs `--force`, a shared file is never replaced, ledger kept off an empty shelf, path escape refused, dry run writes nothing, the sheet's plain words, the phase |

**Invariants it keeps.** A backup only reads — `ark_storage.spool()` is still the
only writer of `/srv/ark/drop/files`, and a restore is the one deliberate
exception, said out loud in its own header. Collections and the book shelf stay
out of a bundle: content, fetched or imported again, not state. An unmeasurable
age renders as "at a time the box could not read", never `0` (§8.7).

**Said out loud, so nobody assumes otherwise:** a copy left on the same card does
not survive that card dying — copying the folder to a stick or a computer is the
owner's job and the sheet says so. No timer (a nightly copy that fills the card
the box reads from is a defect). No button on the owner's page takes or restores a
copy. Bundles are not encrypted; the folder is `0700` because it holds accounts.

**Next work after this checkpoint:** ARK Arcade (§8.11), then stage 7 — the
household layer (emergency content review, the essentials pack, QR cards,
accessibility, the household page). Before any tag: deploy the whole branch and
walk through §8.9 on a phone.

### 8.11 ARK ARCADE — delivered 24 Oct 2026, **deployed 25 Oct 2026, unplayed**

A game shelf on the same box: `http://ark.lan/arcade/`. A guest taps a game and
plays; a **name** is optional and only keeps saves. A name is *not* an
administrator: different tables, different cookie, different origin, and no
path in the arcade can reach `/admin`. Code and docs are on
`feature/admin-hardening`; **the shelf is deployed** (25 Oct 2026) with 23 games on
the card. **No person has played one yet**; a headless Chromium on the Mac has drawn a
Game Boy and a Game Boy Advance game from the box's own files, with real save states
(§18.2), so the serving path is proved and only the hands are missing. **The live source
has never been asked for a file from the box** (the Pi has no route to the internet).
`pytest` **174 passed** at that checkpoint (14 files);
**267** now. Detail: `docs/IMPLEMENTATION-LOG.md` §12, §15, §17 and §18.

| Piece | What it owns |
|---|---|
| `pi/arcade/emulatorjs/` | EmulatorJS 4.2.3 vendored: loader, minified player, five cores, `SHA256SUMS`. No CDN, no external font, no telemetry |
| `pi/arcade/static/`, `templates/`, `catalog/seed.json` | shelf styling/notices, the one page template, and the **23 pinned first games** (10 GB, 6 GBC, 6 GBA, 1 NES) with recorded size + SHA-256 and the licence each source states (provenance, never a gate) |
| `pi/api/ark_arcade.py` | `SYSTEMS`, slug/ROM guards, `rom_facts`, `license_problem`, shelf queries, players/sessions/backoff, and the complete save layer (`SaveConflict`, `SaveRefused`, `_atomic_write`, `player_save_put/get/delete`, export zip, bounded import) |
| `pi/api/ark_arcade_install.py` | the only download path, discovery, seed (from the catalogue **or** from carried files with `--from-dir`), owner decisions, auto-install, temp sweep. **A candidate is not a URL**: its address is rebuilt from a validated slug + filename against the prefix for **its own console** |
| `pi/api/ark_arcade_web.py` | public shelf and game page, ROM/emulator files, player pages and save API, export/import. Player pages redirect to the TLS origin; the API refuses anything not marked as that origin |
| `pi/bin/ark-arcade` | `--seed`, `--discover --auto`, `--install`, `--refuse`, `--allow`, `--list`, `--status`; installed to `/usr/local/sbin` |
| `pi/nginx/ark-players.conf` | `:8445`, TLS only, `/arcade/` + `/assets/` only, `X-ARK-Player-Origin: 1`, `404` for `/admin` |
| `pi/systemd/ark-arcade-discover.*` | 15 min after boot, then weekly; seed is non-fatal, discovery records only; installs only if the owner's switch is on |
| `ark_admin.py` **Games** | shelf + licence + hide/show, suggestions + **Check and add**, both switches, audited |
| `ark-backup` / `ark-restore` | named players' saves (`arcade/saves/…`) travel as state; missing saves are added `0600` in `0700` folders, an existing save is never replaced. The games themselves stay out — content, fetched again |

**The gate chain.** A licence is recorded (never checked) → address rebuilt from
validated slug/filename against **that console's** prefix → source allowlist,
redirects refused, 64 MB cap → temp write + fsync in `/srv/ark/arcade/tmp` →
SHA-256 + size + `rom_facts` → `os.replace` into `games/` → rows in one
transaction (new file unlinked if the DB refuses). A failure leaves the shelf
exactly as it was.

**Saves.** Guest saves live in the browser (EmulatorJS local storage). A named
player gets three server slots per game; folders `0700`, files `0600`. A stale
browser gets **409**, another player gets **404**, a missing page token **403**.
Export is one zip; import accepts only `saves/<game>/<state-NN.state|battery.srm>`,
requires the game on the shelf, and skips anything that climbs.

**The boundary that must not be broken.** `ark_storage.spool()` is still the only
writer of `drop/files`; `ark_arcade_install.publish_candidate()` is the only
writer of `games/`; `player_save_put()` is the only writer of `saves/`. The
public nginx location **blanks** `X-ARK-Player-Origin`; only `:8445` sets it. A
game page must never contain an `http://` or `https://` asset reference — that is
what makes it work with the internet unplugged. Hiding a game is reversible and
deletes nothing; there is no game-delete button on purpose.

**Deliberate gaps, say them out loud:** no player password recovery, no
per-player quota, no save trash, no owner reset/delete for a player name, no
"check now" button (the weekly timer is the only check), **no drop folder and no
upload step for a game you supply yourself** (it goes on with one console command,
`/usr/local/sbin/ark-arcade --import …`, run as `pi`; a zip is not unpacked, there
is no cover art and no delete button), **no catalogue entries at
all for SNES or Genesis** (a game for those consoles must be hand-carried with
`--import`), and **no game has ever been played on the Pi** — the shelf is deployed
and its files are proved, but EmulatorJS has not run on that hardware. Before release: deploy the
branch with `--only 20-storage,30-calibre,60-portal,65-admin,70-network,95-backup`,
then `--only 90-verify`; play a game on a phone with the internet unplugged, make
a name, save/export/import, and watch the first weekly run.

### 8.12 25 OCT HARDENING — delivered, **deployed 25 Oct 2026**

Code, tests and docs are committed on `feature/admin-hardening`; `pytest` is
**267 passed** (15 files). **The whole branch is deployed on the box** (25 Oct
2026) and `90-verify` finished green — see §8.12a. What that does *not* mean: no
human has walked the phone walk-through, the v4 migration has run once (on the
live database) and never on a card that already held v4 rows, and the
bootstrap-password file and the constant-work sign-in have only met the test
suite and one sign-in probe.

| Area | What is true now |
|---|---|
| First sign-in | There is no public bootstrap literal. `ark_security.bootstrap_password()` generates a 20-character password at first start into `/srv/ark/api/data/bootstrap-password` (`0600`), or honours `ARK_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD` when set (tests only). The setup page names the file; the owner reads it over SSH. A wiped database with the install marker still becomes `recovery-required`, never a recreated default |
| Sign-in / recovery | `player_authenticate()` and administrator sign-in verify against `sec.DUMMY_HASH` when the name is unknown, so an unknown name costs the same work as a real one. Password minimum remains 12, maximum 1024, rejected never truncated |
| Setup door | `:8444` is TLS-only, loopback-bound; nginx sets `X-ARK-Setup-Channel: loopback`; `:8443` blanks it; `over_tls()` trusts `X-Forwarded-Proto` only from a trusted peer. `test_tls_probe.py` drives the installer's plain-HTTP verdict against real sockets |
| Schema | **`SCHEMA_VERSION = 4`.** v4 adds `player_game_prefs` (autosave, favourite, last_played) and language/rights/provenance/release/notices evidence on `games`, `acquisition_candidates` and `game_revisions`. Existing candidates with no language or no rights evidence move to `status = 'review'` once. `ALTER TABLE ... DEFAULT ''` keeps old rows readable |
| Arcade eligibility | `language_facts()` allows English, a wordless game and a game whose language nobody recorded; it refuses only a language the source *records* as non-English and a source that contradicts itself. `review_reason()` is the single owner-facing explanation. **A licence is provenance**: `license_problem()` never refuses and `rights_facts()` never refuses (owner's decision, 25 Oct 2026), so an owner-supplied file goes through the same shape/language proof without a licence question |
| Arcade player choices | A named player's favourite sorts first on the shelf; autosave/favourite/last-played are preferences, not saves, and follow the name to another device. A guest sees A–Z and writes nothing |
| Restore | `ark-restore` pauses every writer through `quiesce()`, restarts what it stopped in `finally`, supports `--no-service` for a card mounted in a reader, uses `replace=True` only under `--force` for records and a named player's save, and still never replaces an existing shared file |
| Backup | Manifest still format 1 with per-file sha256; bundles may be compressed; vendor/emulator payloads are handled by the declared vendor rule, never by a path guess. `ark_bundle.verify_bundle()` remains the only verifier. **Both tools now hand a published bundle (and its `.lock`) to the destination folder's owner**, because `sudo ark-backup` left a `root:root 0700` copy that the `pi`-run owner page could not open (see §8.12a and §16.3 of `docs/IMPLEMENTATION-LOG.md`) |

**Still pending:** the phone walk-through in §8.9 — the deploy has happened
(§8.12a). The v4 migration has now run **once, on the live database**, from the
`.backup` copy at `/root/ark-snapshot-20261025-092106`; it has never run on a card
that already held v4 rows. The bootstrap-password file, the constant-work sign-in
and the restore quiesce have still only met the test suite and one sign-in probe,
never a human.

### 8.12a THE DEPLOY — 25 Oct 2026, green

| | |
|---|---|
| Command | `scripts/03-provision.sh ark.local --only 20-storage,30-calibre,60-portal,65-admin,70-network,95-backup`, then `--only 90-verify` |
| Result | both runs `ARK_INSTALL_COMPLETE`; `90-verify` → *“everything a visitor touches is working”* |
| Rollback | `/root/ark-snapshot-20261025-092106` (pointer `/root/ark-snapshot.latest`): `ark.db` via SQLite `.backup`, `api-data.tgz`, `etc-ark.tgz`, old `/etc/nginx`, old `/srv/ark/src` |
| Schema | migrated in place **v1 → v4**; `ark-backup` reports *“Records were at layout version 4”* |
| Readiness | `curl http://ark.local/api/ready` → **200**, `ready 7 / total 8`, `blocking: []` — *as measured at that moment, when §16.2 had not yet counted openable files and the ghost `e2e-drop-test.epub` record passed for content*. After the redeploy the same box answers **503** with `blocking: ["File Box"]` until that record is deleted; see *After the deploy* below and §16.5 |
| Doors | `:80` `/admin` 404; `:8443` 303; `:8444` 303; Community + Games 303; `:8445` 200, `/admin` 404; plain HTTP to both TLS ports rejected 400; management origin hides every public location 404 |
| Book door | `GET /books/` 200, `GET /books/admin` 404, `POST /books/book/1` 403, `POST /books/delete/1` 404, `GET /books/advsearch` 200 — the `must_be` proofs in `60-portal` |
| Units | eleven `enabled` + `active`, including `ark-net-watch.timer`, `ark-book-import.path`, `ark-arcade-discover.timer` |
| First copy | `ark-backup-20261025-093643`, 203.2 kB, *“is whole: 3 files … every fingerprint matches”*; `ark-restore --dry-run` on it refused the occupied box, *“Nothing has been changed”* |
| After the deploy | Two defects found by *reading the pages* — **fixed, tested, committed and now on the box** (25 Oct, `--only 60-portal,95-backup`, then `90-verify`): the owner page counted File Box **records** instead of openable files (§16.2), and the first copy was `root:root 0700` so **Latest copy** said *no copy has been made yet* about a bundle `--verify` had just proved whole (§16.3). Phase `95-backup` adopted `ark-backup-20261025-093643` in place (`pi:pi 700`), **Latest copy** reads it, `90-verify` asks the same question with `runuser -u pi`, and `/api/ready` answers **503 `blocking: ["File Box"]`** while the ghost `e2e-drop-test.epub` record is still there — an empty File Box is ready, one that claims a file it cannot open is not. Deleting that record through Community → Shared files returns the box to 200; nothing deletes it for the owner (§16.5) |

**Two defects the deploy found in its own proofs, and both are now tests:**
`65-admin.sh` proved `:8443` with an IP literal, which sends **no SNI**, so nginx
served the rejecting default server and curl said `000` on a healthy door; the
fix (`ark_tls_code` + `--resolve`) then had to be repeated in `90-verify`, which
had its own copy — the helper now lives in `pi/lib/common.sh` and a test fails if
either step grows a TLS probe of its own. Details and the full evidence table:
`docs/IMPLEMENTATION-LOG.md` §15.

**One thing found on the box, and fixed:** a `files` row (`e2e-drop-test.epub`,
24 Oct, pre-spool test) with no file in `drop/files`, so `/files` offered a
download that answered 404. A row *can* outlive its file by design —
`ark-restore --no-files` restores records onto an empty File Box — so the listing
now asks the folder: `main._present()` filters the list **and its number** to
files that can really be opened, keeps the row (the owner's **Shared files** page
is where a gone file is noticed and deleted, and its audit says *“record removed;
the file was already gone”*), and shows the unfiltered list if the folder itself
cannot be read — an unreadable File Box is not an empty one. Test:
`tests/test_storage.py::test_a_row_whose_file_has_gone_is_not_offered_to_the_reader`.

**Two more found by reading what the box said about itself — fixed and on the box
(25 Oct, `--only 60-portal,95-backup`):** the owner page
counted `files` **rows**, so **What is working** could say *"1 file shared"* while
the portal was empty; and the first bundle taken with `sudo ark-backup` was
`root:root 0700` inside the `pi:pi 0700` copy folder, so **Latest copy** said *no
copy has been made yet* about a bundle `--verify` had just proved whole.
`files_section()` now counts what a reader can **open** (`files`, `files_missing`,
`uploads`; `files_missing: None` when the folder cannot be listed) and says
*"Nothing here can be opened: …"* instead of calling leftover records content;
`ark-backup` hands each bundle and its `.lock` to the **destination folder's
owner** and phase `95-backup` adopts bundles that already exist;
`copies_unreadable()` gives a copy the pages may not open its own sentence rather
than reporting none, and `90-verify` asks the same question as `pi`
(`runuser -u "$ARK_PI_USER" -- test -r …`) so the gate can no longer pass a copy
the page cannot open. Eight tests (§16.4 of `docs/IMPLEMENTATION-LOG.md`).



